Day 12 of #100DaysOfDeepLearning — Batch vs Stochastic Gradient Descent

Today, I explored Batch Gradient Descent vs Stochastic Gradient Descent and understood how the choice of batch size affects the training process of a neural network.

Instead of only learning the definitions, I implemented both approaches on the Social Network Ads dataset and compared their training behaviour.

🔹 Stochastic Gradient Descent: batch_size = 1 — updates the model using one sample at a time, resulting in more frequent but noisier updates.
🔹 Batch Gradient Descent: batch_size = 250 — uses a larger portion of the dataset for each update, producing more stable updates.

I also visualized the loss curves to understand how differently the model converges under different batch sizes.

The main takeaway: batch size is not just a parameter—it directly influences the optimization dynamics, convergence, and training behaviour of a neural network. 🧠

Continuing to build my Deep Learning foundation by understanding the concepts first and then validating them through implementation.

